Z||000002||GTMike||06-28-2001||04:55 AM||||hey thanks for that post. i actually found that page right after i posted my question.<P>the only thing that scares me away from doing my own set up is the electrical.  like finding a place to get the chip and have it programed.  <P>do you think its possible to get only parts of a kit from  a company.  i could find a trubo and rebuild it myself i could also make the exauset after the turbo.  i would like to order the cross over pipe, collecter, oil lines, and the programed chip.<P>any one ever done something like this or think its possible?||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||06-28-2001||07:53 AM||||the full kit runs around $3,000 has everything. I dont recommend you improvise one. Maybe if someone has already experimented and found something that works rite, copy them. I mickey mouse set up is quick way to blow an engine - then you have to start all over again ($$$$$$$$$$$$$)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||06-28-2001||09:40 AM||||It's not really the turbo that is worth the money. It's the plumbing. This stuff is an exact science , A/R ratio's. Dennis has a great page , but dennis also has a lot of knowledge in  the situation. I doubt Design one will sell singular parts , specifically the plumbing. You can pick up a T3 turbo from a tbird for $40 or so , rebuild kit is $75 from <A HREF="http://www.turbocity.com" TARGET=_blank>www.turbocity.com</A>  , they also have all the flanges and what not needed to build the system<P>I used there kit to rebuild the turbo on my merkur.  The cross over pipe is a big issue. if it's not welded by a compitent welder , you will run into a load of headaches.<P>It can be done , but research is the key. I wouldn;t do it as a first time project. Personally I would rather tackle a 3800SC. Lots of reliable ponies , gas milage , no heat , huge aftermarket , 13's out of the box, Blower whine , torque. It's also listed as one of the ten best motors in the world.<P>the turbo is a good motor no doubt , as I'm sure roger can attest to. But it depends on technical ability.<P><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">       <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hartz||06-28-2001||10:43 AM||||Well, I will probably be in the minority on this one, but it's my opinion that the turbos take a lot of time/money/effort to make work right.  They're good fun once you get them sorted, but it is easy to spend a lot of money doing so to solve problems like computers, heat, engine knock, etc.  Plus, depending on the condition of the engine, if you're going to run much over 5-8 lbs. of boost (and you'll need to do so to achieve the numbers you're looking for), you'll probably want to do some engine work to ensure not blowing anything up.  Again, more $$$!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Bottom line - Either option requires money, skill, and patience. If I had to do it over again, I'd look at an engine swap before a turbo.  (and I own one of each)<P>I don't know much about them, but a lot of guys here on the forum are talking up the 4.9L Caddy engine swaps as being cheaper/easier.  I'd again just warn anyone trying anything like this that there are NO easy answers to putting 250-300 HP in a Fiero, but it might be worth checking out.  Some are obviously easier than others, I suppose...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck,<BR>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P><BR>||||reg||1
